# Testing (all languages)

## Test first
1. Write the failing test from the acceptance criteria (RED). Run it and see it fail for the right reason.
2. Write the minimal code to pass (GREEN).
3. Refactor with the tests green.
4. Run the project gate (`VERIFY_CMD` in `.agent-loop.env`, or the commands in `AGENTS.md`). The agent's claim that
   it works never counts; the gate does.

The `implement-task` skill runs one task this way (the Task Loop uses it for every task).

## What to test
- Unit tests for domain logic and utilities; integration tests for endpoints, database access and external
  integrations; end-to-end tests for the critical user flows.
- Aim for 80% line coverage on new code as a floor, not a goal; test behaviour, not implementation details.
- Every bug fix starts with a test that reproduces the bug.

## Structure
- Arrange, Act, Assert. One behaviour per test.
- Names describe the behaviour: `returns_empty_list_when_no_match`, `rejects token with wrong audience`.
- Tests are isolated: no order dependence, no shared mutable state, no real network or clock unless the test is
  about them.
- Test data is synthetic. Never real personal data, client data or production identifiers in tests or fixtures.

## Never
- Delete, skip or weaken a test to make the gate pass. Fix the code; change a test only when the test is wrong,
  and say why in the commit. (The Armature test guard fails the attempt on removed or skipped tests.)

